  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nAs the upper level, positively tilted trough shifts east the Ohio \nValley is positioned downwind of the trough axis. Strong \nsouthwesterly flow continues to bring in moisture and warmer \ntemperatures. Increased pressure gradient and mixing heights above \n6k ft will bring wind gust this afternoon over 25 mph. Winds will \nsubside overnight with 5 - 10 mph southerly winds. Maximum \ntemperatures for this afternoon are expected to be in the low 90s \nacross the area.\n\nThe low pressure that is associated with the upper level troughing, \nbegins to lift into a more zonal flow pattern aloft. A cold front \nwill then begin to push southeast through the area early Friday. \nThis in combination with ample moisture will bring widespread rain \nshowers through the day Friday. Rain chances will increase starting \nin southern IN between 2AM - 5AM, and move SE through the CWA. Ahead \nof the boundary, PWATs over 2 inches will surge into the area. Heavy \nrainfall rates can be expected, however hydro concerns are low. QPF \nvalues will range 0.5\" - 1\" across the CWA. No severe threat is \nexpected with this system as instability is limited and wind shear \nis marginal. \n\nMaximum temperatures on Friday will vary north to south as the \nfrontal boundary will be set over the area. Along and north of the \nOhio River will likely only see highs in the mid 70s, while south \ncentral KY highs in the low 80s. Rain chances will decrease late \nFriday with 30% chances in the southern part of the CWA through \nSaturday morning. \n\nPost frontal boundary, surface high pressure will build in bringing \nnortheasterly flow back to the Ohio Valley. A cooler and drier \nairmass will build back into region making for a pleasant weekend. \nDaytime highs are expected to be in the low to upper 70s with \nnighttime lows in low 50s. Ridging and high pressure will continue \nfor the first half of the week with mostly clear skies and dry \nconditions. Temperatures will look to stay around climatological \nnormals to start the week.",
